Q: Simple example of a Non-Parameterized Constructor
Definition
A Non-Parameterized Constructor in Java is a constructor without any parameters.
It is used to initialize objects with default values when they are created.
If no constructor is defined, Java automatically provides a default constructor.

public class Car {

    String brand;
    int year;

    // Non-Parameterized Constructor
    public Car() {
        // Initializing default values
        brand = "Unknown";
        year = 2000;
        System.out.println("Non-Parameterized Constructor called");
    }

    // Method to display car details
    public void displayInfo() {
        System.out.println("Brand: " + brand);
        System.out.println("Year: " + year);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Creating an object using the non-parameterized constructor
        Car myCar = new Car();

        // Displaying the initialized values
        myCar.displayInfo();
    }
}

OUTPUT:
Non-Parameterized Constructor called
Brand: Unknown
Year: 2000

